IMPORT prelude

-- Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) -- the operative sections, as a PARTY meets them.
--
-- SEAT: a party to a civil claim (typically an injury claim with an interstate side: an accident in another State, a
-- defendant or insurer in another State, a related claim already running in another court) who asks which court can
-- hear the claim, whether it must or may be moved to another court, which law that court will apply, and what can be
-- done about the decision. The Act names no claimant or defendant: both meet the same rules, so "my proceeding" is
-- either side's. Courts, tribunals and Attorneys-General get no seat.
--
-- What the Act decides is mostly left to the court ("it appears to the Supreme Court that ...", "in the opinion of the
-- court", "the interests of justice"). Every such judgment is an input worded as the court's view, never decided here.
-- Terms borrowed from Commonwealth law ("special federal matter", the Schedule to the Commonwealth Act, the Corporations
-- Act Part 9.6A, the Competition and Consumer Act Schedule 2) are asked as facts. The Act sets no time limits, so no
-- regulative rule is written (NOTES.md, Scope).
--
-- No module here imports another subject. Where s 11 points to the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993, its s 5
-- is restated narrowly beside the question that needs it, with the section.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Jurisdiction
-- of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987, Act No. 29/1987, Version No. 026 (as at 1 July 2015), not the authorised version.
-- (c) State of Victoria; published with the authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may
-- be reproduced except in accordance with the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October
-- 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987: a party's questions`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 3 -- the courts and places the Act speaks of`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 3(1) "State includes the Northern Territory and the Australian Capital Territory"; "Territory does not include the
-- Northern Territory or the Australian Capital Territory" (so a "Territory" is one such as Norfolk Island; fork F1).
-- "Family Court means the Family Court of Australia"; "Federal Court means the Federal Court of Australia".
-- "State Family Court" is a court to which s 41 of the Family Law Act 1975 (Cth) applies by Proclamation: the Family
-- Court of Western Australia (borrowed; fork F2). s 3(2): outside s 4(3), "the Supreme Court of a State" includes the
-- State Family Court of that State. "The Supreme Court", unqualified, is the Supreme Court of Victoria.
DECLARE `Court` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court of Victoria`
    `a Victorian court other than the Supreme Court, or a Victorian tribunal`
    `the Supreme Court of another State (New South Wales, Queensland, South Australia, Tasmania, Western Australia, the Northern Territory or the Australian Capital Territory)`
    `the Supreme Court of a Territory (one other than the Northern Territory and the Australian Capital Territory, such as Norfolk Island)`
    `the Family Court of Western Australia (a State Family Court of another State)`
    `the Federal Court of Australia`
    `the Family Court of Australia`
    `a court outside Australia`

-- "the Federal Court or the Family Court"
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 3 -- the Federal Court or the Family Court` `the court` IF
    CONSIDER `the court`
    WHEN `the Federal Court of Australia` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Family Court of Australia` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- "the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory", read with s 3(2) (a State Family Court of another State
-- counts), as ss 5, 8 and 10 use it.
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ss 3(2), 5 -- the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory` `the court` IF
    CONSIDER `the court`
    WHEN `the Supreme Court of another State (New South Wales, Queensland, South Australia, Tasmania, Western Australia, the Northern Territory or the Australian Capital Territory)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Supreme Court of a Territory (one other than the Northern Territory and the Australian Capital Territory, such as Norfolk Island)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Family Court of Western Australia (a State Family Court of another State)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- Places, for s 11 ("the State or Territory in which the court is sitting") and for the Choice of Law (Limitation
-- Periods) Act 1993 s 5 ("another place being another State, a Territory or New Zealand").
DECLARE `Place` IS ONE OF
    `Victoria`
    `another Australian State, the Northern Territory or the Australian Capital Territory`
    `a Territory other than the Northern Territory and the Australian Capital Territory (for example Norfolk Island)`
    `New Zealand`
    `a country other than Australia or New Zealand`

GIVEN `the place` IS A `Place`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 11 -- a State or Territory` `the place` IF
    CONSIDER `the place`
    WHEN `Victoria` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `another Australian State, the Northern Territory or the Australian Capital Territory` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a Territory other than the Northern Territory and the Australian Capital Territory (for example Norfolk Island)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 3, 3A, 4 -- which other courts can hear my Victorian claim`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 3 "State matter means a matter -- (a) in which the Supreme Court has jurisdiction otherwise than by reason of a law
-- of the Commonwealth or of another State; or (b) removed to the Supreme Court under section 8". A "means" definition:
-- TRUE when a limb holds, FALSE otherwise.
GIVEN `the Supreme Court has jurisdiction apart from Commonwealth or other States' law` IS A BOOLEAN
      `removed to the Supreme Court under s 8` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 3 -- a State matter` `the Supreme Court has jurisdiction apart from Commonwealth or other States' law` `removed to the Supreme Court under s 8` IF
        `the Supreme Court has jurisdiction apart from Commonwealth or other States' law`
    OR  `removed to the Supreme Court under s 8`

-- s 3A "This Act does not apply to the jurisdiction of courts with which Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act
-- 2001 of the Commonwealth deals." s 3 "proceeding does not include a criminal proceeding". s 4(3) "The Supreme Court of
-- another State or of a Territory has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction with respect to State
-- matters." s 4(4) the same for "the State Family Court of another State". s 4(5) no jurisdiction "with respect to
-- criminal matters". s 4(1), (2) (vesting in the Federal Court and the Family Court) are repealed (No. 22/2000).
DECLARE `Can that court hear my claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: that Supreme Court has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction with respect to my claim, a State matter (s 4(3))`
    `yes: that State Family Court has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction with respect to my claim, a State matter (s 4(4))`
    `the Supreme Court of Victoria hears a State matter in its own jurisdiction; s 4 vests jurisdiction in the courts of other States and Territories`
    `no: this Act does not apply to jurisdiction dealt with by Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act 2001 (s 3A)`
    `no: this Act vests no jurisdiction with respect to criminal matters (ss 3, 4(5))`
    `no: my claim is not a State matter (s 3), so s 4 vests nothing for it`
    `not covered: this Act no longer vests Victorian jurisdiction in the Federal Court or the Family Court (s 4(1), (2) repealed in 2000)`
    `not covered: s 4 vests jurisdiction only in the Supreme Courts and State Family Courts of other States and Territories`

GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court`
      `it is a criminal matter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Supreme Court has jurisdiction apart from Commonwealth or other States' law` IS A BOOLEAN
      `removed to the Supreme Court under s 8` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a Corporations Act Part 9.6A Division 1 matter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can that court hear my claim`
`ss 3, 3A, 4 -- that court can hear my claim` `the court` `it is a criminal matter` `the Supreme Court has jurisdiction apart from Commonwealth or other States' law` `removed to the Supreme Court under s 8` `a Corporations Act Part 9.6A Division 1 matter` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `a Corporations Act Part 9.6A Division 1 matter`
        THEN `no: this Act does not apply to jurisdiction dealt with by Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act 2001 (s 3A)`
        IF `it is a criminal matter`
        THEN `no: this Act vests no jurisdiction with respect to criminal matters (ss 3, 4(5))`
        IF NOT (`s 3 -- a State matter` `the Supreme Court has jurisdiction apart from Commonwealth or other States' law` `removed to the Supreme Court under s 8`)
        THEN `no: my claim is not a State matter (s 3), so s 4 vests nothing for it`
        IF `the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Victoria`
        THEN `the Supreme Court of Victoria hears a State matter in its own jurisdiction; s 4 vests jurisdiction in the courts of other States and Territories`
        IF `the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of another State (New South Wales, Queensland, South Australia, Tasmania, Western Australia, the Northern Territory or the Australian Capital Territory)`
        THEN `yes: that Supreme Court has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction with respect to my claim, a State matter (s 4(3))`
        IF `the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of a Territory (one other than the Northern Territory and the Australian Capital Territory, such as Norfolk Island)`
        THEN `yes: that Supreme Court has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction with respect to my claim, a State matter (s 4(3))`
        IF `the court` EQUALS `the Family Court of Western Australia (a State Family Court of another State)`
        THEN `yes: that State Family Court has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction with respect to my claim, a State matter (s 4(4))`
        IF `s 3 -- the Federal Court or the Family Court` `the court`
        THEN `not covered: this Act no longer vests Victorian jurisdiction in the Federal Court or the Family Court (s 4(1), (2) repealed in 2000)`
        OTHERWISE `not covered: s 4 vests jurisdiction only in the Supreme Courts and State Family Courts of other States and Territories`

@export Can the court I name hear my claim under Victorian law by cross-vesting (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) ss 3, 3A, 4)? The Supreme Court of every other State and Territory, and the Family Court of Western Australia, has the Supreme Court of Victoria's jurisdiction over "State matters": civil matters the Supreme Court of Victoria could hear under Victorian law alone, or ones removed into it under s 8. Not criminal matters, and not the Corporations Act matters s 3A excludes.
GIVEN `court` IS A `Court` @desc The court you ask about: the Supreme Court of Victoria; a Victorian court other than the Supreme Court, or a Victorian tribunal; the Supreme Court of another State (including the Northern Territory and the ACT); the Supreme Court of a Territory such as Norfolk Island; the Family Court of Western Australia; the Federal Court of Australia; the Family Court of Australia; or a court outside Australia
      `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter is a criminal one (a prosecution) rather than a civil claim
      `Victorian jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court of Victoria could hear your claim under its own jurisdiction, apart from any Commonwealth law or any other State's law (for a personal injury claim in negligence, usually yes)
      `removed under s 8` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim was removed into the Supreme Court of Victoria from a lower court or tribunal by an order under s 8 of this Act
      `Corporations Part 9.6A` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter is one whose jurisdiction Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth) deals with (civil matters arising under the Corporations legislation); for an injury claim, usually no
GIVETH A `Can that court hear my claim`
`Can that court hear my Victorian claim` MEANS
    `ss 3, 3A, 4 -- that court can hear my claim` `court` `criminal` `Victorian jurisdiction` `removed under s 8` `Corporations Part 9.6A`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 5 -- moving my proceeding to the more appropriate court`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 5(7) "A court may transfer a proceeding under this section on the application of a party to the proceeding, of its
-- own motion or on the application of the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory." s 3 "party
-- ... includes a person who intervenes in the proceeding"; "proceeding does not include a criminal proceeding".
-- A party may apply: TRUE. A criminal proceeding is no "proceeding" (a flat negative): FALSE. Anyone else: the list is
-- of who may apply, not closed with "only"; a non-party, non-Attorney-General is not named: NOTHING (fork F3).
GIVEN `I am a party or an intervener` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is a criminal proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(7) -- I may apply for a transfer` `I am a party or an intervener` `it is a criminal proceeding` MEANS
    IF `it is a criminal proceeding`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `I am a party or an intervener`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING

@export Can I ask the court to transfer my proceeding to another court under s 5 (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 5(7))? A party (including someone who intervened) may apply; the court may also transfer of its own motion, or on the application of an Attorney-General. Criminal proceedings are outside the Act. No answer for someone who is neither a party nor an Attorney-General.
GIVEN `party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party to the proceeding (a plaintiff, a defendant, or someone who has intervened in it)
      `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding is a criminal proceeding
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I ask for my proceeding to be transferred` MEANS
    `s 5(7) -- I may apply for a transfer` `party` `criminal`

-- s 5(1): pending in the Supreme Court; "it appears to the Supreme Court that ... (ii) having regard to (A) [whether it
-- could have been brought only in the Federal or Family Court], (B) [how far it turns on Commonwealth law outside the
-- Supreme Court's own jurisdiction] and (C) the interests of justice -- it is more appropriate that the relevant
-- proceeding be determined by the Federal Court or the Family Court" -> "the Supreme Court shall transfer". Limbs (i)
-- and (iii) are repealed (No. 22/2000): no related-proceeding or bare interests-of-justice limb is left.
-- s 5(2): pending in the Supreme Court; (i) related to a proceeding pending in the Supreme Court of another State or of a
-- Territory and more appropriate there; (ii) more appropriate there having regard to (A), (B), (C); or (iii) "otherwise
-- in the interests of justice" -> "the first court shall transfer".
-- s 5(3): the mirror, from the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory to the Supreme Court of Victoria.
-- s 5(4): from the Federal Court or the Family Court to the Supreme Court: (i) related and more appropriate, (ii) having
-- regard to (A)-(D), or (iii) otherwise in the interests of justice.
-- "Where ... it appears ... shall": when the court's view is not one of the limbs, s 5 does not require the transfer
-- (a "where" condition: the "no" kind). Between other pairs of courts this Act's s 5 provides nothing (NOTHING kind; the
-- Commonwealth's or another State's cross-vesting Act may).
DECLARE `Must it be transferred` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court of Victoria must transfer it to the Federal Court or the Family Court (s 5(1))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court of Victoria must transfer it to that other Supreme Court (s 5(2))`
    `yes: that other Supreme Court must transfer it to the Supreme Court of Victoria (s 5(3))`
    `yes: the Federal Court or the Family Court must transfer it to the Supreme Court of Victoria (s 5(4))`
    `no: on the court's view of these facts, s 5 does not require this transfer`
    `not covered: this Act's s 5 provides no transfer between these two courts (the Commonwealth's or another State's cross-vesting Act may)`

GIVEN `the court it is in` IS A `Court`
      `the court it would go to` IS A `Court`
      `the court finds a related proceeding there makes that court more appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court finds that court more appropriate on jurisdiction, the law involved and the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court finds it otherwise in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Must it be transferred`
`s 5(1)-(4) -- it must be transferred` `the court it is in` `the court it would go to` `the court finds a related proceeding there makes that court more appropriate` `the court finds that court more appropriate on jurisdiction, the law involved and the interests of justice` `the court finds it otherwise in the interests of justice` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF      `the court it is in` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Victoria`
            AND `s 3 -- the Federal Court or the Family Court` `the court it would go to`
        THEN IF `the court finds that court more appropriate on jurisdiction, the law involved and the interests of justice`
             THEN `yes: the Supreme Court of Victoria must transfer it to the Federal Court or the Family Court (s 5(1))`
             ELSE `no: on the court's view of these facts, s 5 does not require this transfer`
        IF      `the court it is in` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Victoria`
            AND `ss 3(2), 5 -- the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory` `the court it would go to`
        THEN IF `any limb of s 5(2), (3) or (4)`
             THEN `yes: the Supreme Court of Victoria must transfer it to that other Supreme Court (s 5(2))`
             ELSE `no: on the court's view of these facts, s 5 does not require this transfer`
        IF      `ss 3(2), 5 -- the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory` `the court it is in`
            AND `the court it would go to` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Victoria`
        THEN IF `any limb of s 5(2), (3) or (4)`
             THEN `yes: that other Supreme Court must transfer it to the Supreme Court of Victoria (s 5(3))`
             ELSE `no: on the court's view of these facts, s 5 does not require this transfer`
        IF      `s 3 -- the Federal Court or the Family Court` `the court it is in`
            AND `the court it would go to` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Victoria`
        THEN IF `any limb of s 5(2), (3) or (4)`
             THEN `yes: the Federal Court or the Family Court must transfer it to the Supreme Court of Victoria (s 5(4))`
             ELSE `no: on the court's view of these facts, s 5 does not require this transfer`
        OTHERWISE `not covered: this Act's s 5 provides no transfer between these two courts (the Commonwealth's or another State's cross-vesting Act may)`
    WHERE
        `any limb of s 5(2), (3) or (4)` MEANS
                `the court finds a related proceeding there makes that court more appropriate`
            OR  `the court finds that court more appropriate on jurisdiction, the law involved and the interests of justice`
            OR  `the court finds it otherwise in the interests of justice`

@export Must my proceeding be moved to the court I name (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 5(1)-(4))? Between the Supreme Court of Victoria and the Supreme Court of another State or Territory (either way), and from the Federal Court or the Family Court to the Supreme Court of Victoria, the court must transfer the proceeding if it finds a related proceeding in the other court makes that court more appropriate, or that court is more appropriate having regard to where the case could have been brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice, or that the transfer is otherwise in the interests of justice. From the Supreme Court of Victoria to the Federal Court or the Family Court only the second ground is left. Whether a ground is made out is the court's judgment: give it as the court's view. A transfer gives no court jurisdiction it would not otherwise have (s 5(9)).
GIVEN `now in` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceeding is in now: the Supreme Court of Victoria; a Victorian court other than the Supreme Court, or a Victorian tribunal; the Supreme Court of another State (including the Northern Territory and the ACT); the Supreme Court of a Territory such as Norfolk Island; the Family Court of Western Australia; the Federal Court of Australia; the Family Court of Australia; or a court outside Australia
      `to` IS A `Court` @desc The court it would be transferred to, from the same list
      `related proceeding view` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that your proceeding arises out of, or is related to, another proceeding pending in the court it would go to, and that it is more appropriate for that court to decide it (for example your claim and the other driver's claim over the same accident)
      `more appropriate view` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that court more appropriate, having regard to whether the proceeding (or a substantial part) could only have been brought there, how far it turns on that court's law, and the interests of justice
      `interests of justice view` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds it otherwise in the interests of justice that the other court decide it (for example where the witnesses, the evidence and the governing law are there)
GIVETH A `Must it be transferred`
`Must my proceeding be transferred to that court` MEANS
    `s 5(1)-(4) -- it must be transferred` `now in` `to` `related proceeding view` `more appropriate view` `interests of justice view`

-- s 5(6) "Where (a) a court ... transfers a proceeding to another court under a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of
-- jurisdiction; and (b) it appears to the first court that (i) there is another proceeding pending in the first court
-- that arises out of, or is related to, the first-mentioned proceeding; and (ii) it is in the interests of justice that
-- the other proceeding be determined by the other court -- the first court shall transfer the other proceeding".
-- "Where": TRUE (it must follow) when all hold, FALSE otherwise.
GIVEN `a proceeding was transferred out of this court under a cross-vesting law` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my proceeding in the same court arises out of or relates to it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court finds it in the interests of justice that the other court decide mine too` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(6) -- my related proceeding must follow` `a proceeding was transferred out of this court under a cross-vesting law` `my proceeding in the same court arises out of or relates to it` `the court finds it in the interests of justice that the other court decide mine too` MEANS
    IF      `a proceeding was transferred out of this court under a cross-vesting law`
        AND `my proceeding in the same court arises out of or relates to it`
        AND `the court finds it in the interests of justice that the other court decide mine too`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

@export One proceeding has been transferred to another court under a cross-vesting law. Must my related proceeding, still in the court it left, be transferred to the same court (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 5(6))? Yes, if it arises out of or is related to the transferred one and the court finds it in the interests of justice that the other court decide it.
GIVEN `one was transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proceeding has been transferred out of this court to another court under a cross-vesting law (this Act or the Commonwealth's or another State's)
      `mine is related` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding, still pending in the same court, arises out of or is related to the transferred one
      `interests of justice view` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds it in the interests of justice that the other court decide your proceeding too
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my related proceeding go with it` MEANS
    `s 5(6) -- my related proceeding must follow` `one was transferred` `mine is related` `interests of justice view`

-- s 5(8) "An Australian lawyer who is entitled to practise in a court has, if a proceeding ... in that court is
-- transferred to another court under a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction, the same entitlement to
-- practise in relation to (a) the transferred proceeding; and (b) any other proceeding out of which the transferred
-- proceeding arises or to which [it] is related, being another proceeding that is to be determined together with [it]
-- -- in the other court that the lawyer would have if the other court were a federal court exercising federal
-- jurisdiction." TRUE: the entitlement. "if ... transferred" unmet, or a proceeding outside (a) and (b): FALSE. A lawyer
-- not entitled to practise in the first court is outside the subsection's subject: NOTHING (fork F4).
GIVEN `my lawyer may practise in the court it left` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was transferred under a cross-vesting law` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is the transferred proceeding or a related one heard together with it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(8) -- my lawyer keeps the right to act` `my lawyer may practise in the court it left` `it was transferred under a cross-vesting law` `it is the transferred proceeding or a related one heard together with it` MEANS
    IF NOT (`my lawyer may practise in the court it left`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `it was transferred under a cross-vesting law` AND `it is the transferred proceeding or a related one heard together with it`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

@export Can my lawyer keep acting for me after my proceeding is transferred to a court in another State or a federal court (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 5(8))? A lawyer entitled to practise in the court the proceeding left has, in the new court, the entitlement they would have if it were a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction, for the transferred proceeding and any related one decided together with it. What that entitlement is comes from Commonwealth law, not this Act.
GIVEN `lawyer entitled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your lawyer is an Australian lawyer entitled to practise in the court your proceeding was in
      `transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding was transferred to another court under a cross-vesting law
      `this proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding you ask about is the transferred one, or one it arises out of or relates to that is to be decided together with it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my lawyer keep acting after the transfer` MEANS
    `s 5(8) -- my lawyer keeps the right to act` `lawyer entitled` `transferred` `this proceeding`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 6, 6A -- special federal matters` -- arises only where my proceeding raises a special federal matter
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 3 "special federal matter has the same meaning as in the Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 of the
-- Commonwealth" (borrowed; asked by paragraph of that definition, which s 6(2) and s 6A(1) use).
DECLARE `Special federal matter` IS ONE OF
    `no special federal matter`
    `a special federal matter in paragraph (a), (b) or (d) of the Commonwealth definition`
    `a special federal matter in paragraph (c) or (e) of the Commonwealth definition`
    `a special federal matter in paragraph (ab) of the Commonwealth definition`

-- s 6(1) "If (a) a matter for determination in a proceeding that is pending in the Supreme Court is a special federal
-- matter; and (b) the court does not make an order under subsection (3) ... the court must transfer the proceeding ... to
-- the Federal Court or a court mentioned in subsection (2)(b)." (1A) only "so much of the proceeding as is ... within the
-- jurisdiction (including the accrued jurisdiction)" of that court. (2)(a) paragraphs (a)-(e): the Federal Court; (2)(b)
-- paragraph (ab): "whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the
-- Northern Territory, in the opinion of the court, is appropriate". (9) s 6 does not apply to an appeal in the Full
-- Court where the court below made an order under (3).
-- s 6A(1) applies where (a) the matter is in paragraph (c) or (e); (b) it involves functions or powers conferred on a
-- Commonwealth authority or officer by a State enactment (ADJR Act s 3(1) "enactment" (ca), (cb)); (c) it arises out of
-- or relates to a "State matter proceeding" pending in any court of this State (arising under that enactment, with no
-- (c) or (e) matter). (2) in the Federal Court or the Family Court: it "may transfer the proceeding to the Supreme Court"
-- in the interests of justice; s 5(4) does not apply. (3) in the Supreme Court, with the State matter proceeding pending
-- in a court of this State: "neither section 5(1) nor section 6 applies to require" the transfer; it "may do so if it
-- considers that to be appropriate".
DECLARE `What s 6 does with my proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `s 6 does not apply: no matter in my proceeding is a special federal matter`
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer it to the Federal Court, but only so much of it as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction, including its accrued jurisdiction (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(a))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer it to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it thinks appropriate, but only so much as is within that court's jurisdiction (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(b))`
    `no: the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it determine the proceeding itself`
    `no: s 6 does not apply to this appeal in the Full Court, because the court below ordered under s 6(3) that it determine the matter (s 6(9))`
    `no duty: neither s 5(1) nor s 6 requires the Supreme Court to transfer it, but the Supreme Court may if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice, including keeping related proceedings together (s 6A(3))`
    `the Federal Court or the Family Court may transfer it to the Supreme Court of Victoria if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice, including keeping related proceedings together; s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2))`
    `s 6 does not apply: my proceeding is not pending in the Supreme Court of Victoria`

GIVEN `the court it is in` IS A `Court`
      `the special federal matter` IS A `Special federal matter`
      `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer acting under a State enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a related State matter proceeding is pending in a Victorian court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it determine it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is an appeal in the Full Court after an s 6(3) order below` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What s 6 does with my proceeding`
`ss 6, 6A -- what happens to my special federal matter` `the court it is in` `the special federal matter` `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer acting under a State enactment` `a related State matter proceeding is pending in a Victorian court` `the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it determine it` `it is an appeal in the Full Court after an s 6(3) order below` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the special federal matter` EQUALS `no special federal matter`
        THEN `s 6 does not apply: no matter in my proceeding is a special federal matter`
        IF `s 3 -- the Federal Court or the Family Court` `the court it is in` AND `s 6A(1) applies`
        THEN `the Federal Court or the Family Court may transfer it to the Supreme Court of Victoria if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice, including keeping related proceedings together; s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2))`
        IF NOT (`the court it is in` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Victoria`)
        THEN `s 6 does not apply: my proceeding is not pending in the Supreme Court of Victoria`
        IF `s 6A(1) applies`
        THEN `no duty: neither s 5(1) nor s 6 requires the Supreme Court to transfer it, but the Supreme Court may if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice, including keeping related proceedings together (s 6A(3))`
        IF `it is an appeal in the Full Court after an s 6(3) order below`
        THEN `no: s 6 does not apply to this appeal in the Full Court, because the court below ordered under s 6(3) that it determine the matter (s 6(9))`
        IF `the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it determine it`
        THEN `no: the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it determine the proceeding itself`
        IF `the special federal matter` EQUALS `a special federal matter in paragraph (ab) of the Commonwealth definition`
        THEN `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer it to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it thinks appropriate, but only so much as is within that court's jurisdiction (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer it to the Federal Court, but only so much of it as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction, including its accrued jurisdiction (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(a))`
    WHERE
        `s 6A(1) applies` MEANS
                `the special federal matter` EQUALS `a special federal matter in paragraph (c) or (e) of the Commonwealth definition`
            AND `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer acting under a State enactment`
            AND `a related State matter proceeding is pending in a Victorian court`

@export My proceeding raises a "special federal matter" (as the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act defines it). Must it be moved out of the Supreme Court of Victoria, and to which court (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) ss 6, 6A)? Unless the Supreme Court orders that it decide the matter itself, it must transfer the part within the other court's jurisdiction: to the Federal Court, or for a paragraph (ab) matter to the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory. s 6A relaxes this where a Commonwealth officer acts under a State law and a related State-law proceeding is running in a Victorian court.
GIVEN `now in` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceeding is in now: the Supreme Court of Victoria; a Victorian court other than the Supreme Court, or a Victorian tribunal; the Supreme Court of another State (including the Northern Territory and the ACT); the Supreme Court of a Territory such as Norfolk Island; the Family Court of Western Australia; the Federal Court of Australia; the Family Court of Australia; or a court outside Australia
      `special federal matter` IS A `Special federal matter` @desc Whether a matter in your proceeding is a special federal matter under s 3(1) of the Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Cth), and under which paragraph of that definition: none; (a), (b) or (d); (c) or (e); or (ab)
      `Commonwealth officer under State law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter involves functions or powers given to a Commonwealth authority or officer by a State law of the kind in paragraph (ca) or (cb) of the definition of "enactment" in the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977 (Cth)
      `related State proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter arises out of or relates to another proceeding pending in a Victorian court that arises (wholly or substantially) under that State law and raises no paragraph (c) or (e) matter
      `kept by s 6(3) order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it decide the proceeding itself
      `appeal after s 6(3) order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is an appeal in the Full Court (the Court of Appeal) from a court that made an s 6(3) order about the special federal matter
GIVETH A `What s 6 does with my proceeding`
`Must my special federal matter be transferred` MEANS
    `ss 6, 6A -- what happens to my special federal matter` `now in` `special federal matter` `Commonwealth officer under State law` `related State proceeding` `kept by s 6(3) order` `appeal after s 6(3) order`

-- s 6(3) "The Supreme Court may order that the proceeding be determined by that Court if it is satisfied that there are
-- special reasons for doing so in the particular circumstances of the proceeding other than reasons relevant to the
-- convenience of the parties." (4) "Before making an order under subsection (3), the court must be satisfied that (a) a
-- written notice specifying the nature of the special federal matter has been given to the Attorney-General of the
-- Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the State; and (b) a reasonable time has elapsed". (5) it may adjourn, and
-- may direct a party to give the notice. (6) it must have regard to the general rule that special federal matters go
-- to the Federal Court or a (2)(b) court, and to any Attorney-General's submission.
DECLARE `Can the Supreme Court keep it` IS ONE OF
    `yes, it may order that it decide the proceeding itself (s 6(3)); whether it does is for the court, which must weigh the general rule that special federal matters go to the federal or family courts and any Attorney-General's submission (s 6(6))`
    `no: the Supreme Court may keep it only for special reasons other than the parties' convenience (s 6(3))`
    `not yet: written notice of the special federal matter must first be given to the Attorneys-General of the Commonwealth and of Victoria (s 6(4)(a)); the court may direct a party to give it (s 6(5)(b))`
    `not yet: a reasonable time must first pass for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions (s 6(4)(b)); the court may adjourn meanwhile (s 6(5)(a))`

GIVEN `the court finds special reasons other than the parties' convenience` IS A BOOLEAN
      `notice was given to both Attorneys-General` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court finds a reasonable time has passed since the notice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can the Supreme Court keep it`
`s 6(3)-(6) -- the Supreme Court can keep it` `the court finds special reasons other than the parties' convenience` `notice was given to both Attorneys-General` `the court finds a reasonable time has passed since the notice` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the court finds special reasons other than the parties' convenience`)
        THEN `no: the Supreme Court may keep it only for special reasons other than the parties' convenience (s 6(3))`
        IF NOT (`notice was given to both Attorneys-General`)
        THEN `not yet: written notice of the special federal matter must first be given to the Attorneys-General of the Commonwealth and of Victoria (s 6(4)(a)); the court may direct a party to give it (s 6(5)(b))`
        IF NOT (`the court finds a reasonable time has passed since the notice`)
        THEN `not yet: a reasonable time must first pass for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions (s 6(4)(b)); the court may adjourn meanwhile (s 6(5)(a))`
        OTHERWISE `yes, it may order that it decide the proceeding itself (s 6(3)); whether it does is for the court, which must weigh the general rule that special federal matters go to the federal or family courts and any Attorney-General's submission (s 6(6))`

@export Can the Supreme Court of Victoria keep and decide my proceeding although it raises a special federal matter (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 6(3)-(6))? Only if it is satisfied there are special reasons other than the parties' convenience, and only after written notice to the Commonwealth and Victorian Attorneys-General and a reasonable time for them to consider making submissions. Give the court's findings as inputs.
GIVEN `special reasons view` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds special reasons, in the particular circumstances, for deciding it itself, other than reasons of the parties' convenience
      `notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether written notice specifying the nature of the special federal matter has been given to both the Commonwealth Attorney-General and the Victorian Attorney-General
      `reasonable time view` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that a reasonable time has passed since that notice for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions
GIVETH A `Can the Supreme Court keep it`
`Can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter` MEANS
    `s 6(3)-(6) -- the Supreme Court can keep it` `special reasons view` `notice given` `reasonable time view`

-- s 6(7) "Nothing in this section prevents the court granting urgent relief of an interlocutory nature if it is in the
-- interests of justice to do so." TRUE where the relief is urgent and interlocutory and the court finds it in the
-- interests of justice; FALSE where the "if" fails; NOTHING for relief that is not urgent and interlocutory (s 6(7) is
-- silent; fork F5).
GIVEN `the relief is urgent and interlocutory` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court finds it in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 6(7) -- urgent interlocutory relief is not prevented` `the relief is urgent and interlocutory` `the court finds it in the interests of justice` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the relief is urgent and interlocutory`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `the court finds it in the interests of justice`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

@export My proceeding in the Supreme Court of Victoria raises a special federal matter. Can the Supreme Court still give me urgent interim relief (for example an injunction) before it is transferred (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 6(7))? s 6 does not prevent urgent relief of an interlocutory nature if it is in the interests of justice. No answer for other relief.
GIVEN `urgent interlocutory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the relief you seek is urgent and interlocutory (interim, pending the final decision)
      `interests of justice view` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds it in the interests of justice to grant it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Supreme Court still give me urgent interim relief` MEANS
    `s 6(7) -- urgent interlocutory relief is not prevented` `urgent interlocutory` `interests of justice view`

-- s 6(8) "If, through inadvertence, the Supreme Court determines a proceeding of the kind mentioned in subsection (1)
-- without (a) the court making an order under subsection (3) ...; or (b) a notice mentioned in subsection (4) being
-- given -- nothing in this section invalidates the decision of that court." The question asks whether s 6 makes the
-- decision invalid: FALSE (a flat negative) where it was through inadvertence; NOTHING where it was not (fork F6).
GIVEN `it was decided through inadvertence without the s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 6(8) -- s 6 invalidates the decision` `it was decided through inadvertence without the s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice` MEANS
    IF `it was decided through inadvertence without the s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

@export The Supreme Court of Victoria decided my proceeding although it raised a special federal matter, without an s 6(3) order or the notice to the Attorneys-General. Does s 6 make its decision invalid (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 6(8))? No, if that happened through inadvertence. The Act does not say what follows if it was not inadvertent.
GIVEN `inadvertent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court decided it through inadvertence (by oversight) without the s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the decision invalid because the s 6 steps were missed` MEANS
    `s 6(8) -- s 6 invalidates the decision` `inadvertent`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 7 -- where an appeal goes` -- arises only after a decision I want to appeal
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 3 "Full Court, in relation to a Supreme Court of a State, includes any court of the State to which appeals lie from
-- a single judge of that Supreme Court" (in Victoria, the Court of Appeal).
-- s 7(1) no appeal from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court to the Full Court of the Supreme Court;
-- (2) none from either of those courts to the other. (3) an appeal from a single judge of the Supreme Court where the
-- only matters are not matters arising under an Act in the Schedule to the Commonwealth Act: "instituted only in, and
-- ... determined only by, the Full Court of the Supreme Court". (4) where a matter arises under a Schedule Act: "only
-- ... (a) the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires; or (b) with special leave of
-- the High Court, the High Court". (5) an appeal from a judge of a State Family Court involving a Schedule Act matter
-- and another matter "may be dealt with as if no matter ... were a matter arising under" a Schedule Act. (6) if the Full
-- Court of the Supreme Court has begun to hear an appeal that turns out to be under (4), it "shall, unless the
-- interests of justice require that the Court proceed to determine the proceeding, transfer" it. (7) its decision in
-- that case, or through inadvertence, is not invalidated.
DECLARE `The decision appealed from` IS ONE OF
    `a single judge of the Supreme Court of Victoria`
    `a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court`
    `a judge of the Family Court of Western Australia (a State Family Court)`
    `another court`

DECLARE `Where my appeal goes` IS ONE OF
    `only to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of Victoria (the Court of Appeal) (s 7(3))`
    `only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires, or with special leave to the High Court (s 7(4))`
    `the Full Court of the Supreme Court has begun to hear it, so it must transfer it to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, unless the interests of justice require it to go on (s 7(6))`
    `the Full Court of the Supreme Court has begun to hear it and the interests of justice require it to go on: it may decide it, and its decision is not invalid (s 7(6), (7))`
    `not to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of Victoria, and not to the other of the Federal Court and the Family Court (s 7(1), (2)); where it does go is outside this Act`
    `it may be dealt with as if no matter in it arose under an Act in the Schedule to the Commonwealth Act (s 7(5)); where it goes is outside this Act`
    `not covered: s 7 does not deal with an appeal from this decision`

GIVEN `appealed from` IS A `The decision appealed from`
      `a matter arises under a Schedule Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `another matter is also involved` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Full Court of the Supreme Court has begun to hear it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the interests of justice require the Full Court to go on` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Where my appeal goes`
`s 7 -- where my appeal goes` `appealed from` `a matter arises under a Schedule Act` `another matter is also involved` `the Full Court of the Supreme Court has begun to hear it` `the interests of justice require the Full Court to go on` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `appealed from` EQUALS `a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court`
        THEN `not to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of Victoria, and not to the other of the Federal Court and the Family Court (s 7(1), (2)); where it does go is outside this Act`
        IF `appealed from` EQUALS `a judge of the Family Court of Western Australia (a State Family Court)`
        THEN IF `a matter arises under a Schedule Act` AND `another matter is also involved`
             THEN `it may be dealt with as if no matter in it arose under an Act in the Schedule to the Commonwealth Act (s 7(5)); where it goes is outside this Act`
             ELSE `not covered: s 7 does not deal with an appeal from this decision`
        IF `appealed from` EQUALS `a single judge of the Supreme Court of Victoria`
        THEN IF NOT (`a matter arises under a Schedule Act`)
             THEN `only to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of Victoria (the Court of Appeal) (s 7(3))`
             ELSE IF `the Full Court of the Supreme Court has begun to hear it`
                  THEN IF `the interests of justice require the Full Court to go on`
                       THEN `the Full Court of the Supreme Court has begun to hear it and the interests of justice require it to go on: it may decide it, and its decision is not invalid (s 7(6), (7))`
                       ELSE `the Full Court of the Supreme Court has begun to hear it, so it must transfer it to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, unless the interests of justice require it to go on (s 7(6))`
                  ELSE `only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires, or with special leave to the High Court (s 7(4))`
        OTHERWISE `not covered: s 7 does not deal with an appeal from this decision`

@export Where must my appeal be brought (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 7)? From a single judge of the Supreme Court of Victoria an appeal goes only to its Full Court (the Court of Appeal), unless a matter in it arises under an Act listed in the Schedule to the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act; then only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or the Family Court, or with special leave to the High Court. No appeal lies from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court to the Supreme Court's Full Court. Which Acts are in the Schedule is Commonwealth law: say whether yours is.
GIVEN `appealed from` IS A `The decision appealed from` @desc Whose decision you want to appeal: a single judge of the Supreme Court of Victoria; a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court; a judge of the Family Court of Western Australia; or another court
      `Schedule matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter for decision in the appeal arises under an Act listed in the Schedule to the Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Cth); for an ordinary injury claim under State law, usually no
      `other matter too` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the appeal also involves another matter, not arising under a Schedule Act
      `Full Court began` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Full Court of the Supreme Court of Victoria (the Court of Appeal) has already begun to hear the appeal
      `interests of justice view` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that Court finds that the interests of justice require it to go on and decide the appeal itself
GIVETH A `Where my appeal goes`
`Where does my appeal go` MEANS
    `s 7 -- where my appeal goes` `appealed from` `Schedule matter` `other matter too` `Full Court began` `interests of justice view`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 8, 10 -- moving a proceeding between the Supreme Court and the lower courts`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 8(1) "Where (a) a proceeding ... is pending in (i) a court, other than the Supreme Court; or (ii) a tribunal
-- established by or under an Act; and (b) it appears to the Supreme Court that (i) the relevant proceeding arises out
-- of, or is related to, another proceeding pending in the Federal Court, the Family Court or the Supreme Court of another
-- State or of a Territory and, if an order is made ..., there would be grounds on which that other proceeding could be
-- transferred to the Supreme Court; or (ii) an order should be made ... so that consideration can be given to whether
-- the relevant proceeding should be transferred to another court -- the Supreme Court may, on the application of a
-- party ... or of its own motion, make an order removing the relevant proceeding to the Supreme Court." (2) the Act then
-- applies as if it were pending in the Supreme Court; (3) the Supreme Court may remit it. "A court" and "an Act" are
-- read as Victorian (fork F7).
DECLARE `Can it be removed into the Supreme Court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court may remove it into itself, on my application or of its own motion (s 8(1)); it is then treated as pending in the Supreme Court (s 8(2)), which may later send it back (s 8(3))`
    `no: on the Supreme Court's view of these facts, s 8 does not allow removal`
    `not covered: s 8 removes only a proceeding pending in a court other than the Supreme Court or in a tribunal established under an Act`

GIVEN `it is pending in a Victorian lower court or tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Supreme Court finds a related proceeding elsewhere could then be transferred to it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Supreme Court finds it should be removed so a transfer can be considered` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can it be removed into the Supreme Court`
`s 8 -- it can be removed into the Supreme Court` `it is pending in a Victorian lower court or tribunal` `the Supreme Court finds a related proceeding elsewhere could then be transferred to it` `the Supreme Court finds it should be removed so a transfer can be considered` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`it is pending in a Victorian lower court or tribunal`)
        THEN `not covered: s 8 removes only a proceeding pending in a court other than the Supreme Court or in a tribunal established under an Act`
        IF `the Supreme Court finds a related proceeding elsewhere could then be transferred to it` OR `the Supreme Court finds it should be removed so a transfer can be considered`
        THEN `yes: the Supreme Court may remove it into itself, on my application or of its own motion (s 8(1)); it is then treated as pending in the Supreme Court (s 8(2)), which may later send it back (s 8(3))`
        OTHERWISE `no: on the Supreme Court's view of these facts, s 8 does not allow removal`

@export My proceeding is in a Victorian court below the Supreme Court (such as the County Court or the Magistrates' Court) or in a tribunal. Can it be removed into the Supreme Court of Victoria so that it can be heard with, or transferred to, a related case in another State or a federal court (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 8)? The Supreme Court may remove it, on a party's application or its own motion, if it finds that a related proceeding in the Federal Court, the Family Court or another State's Supreme Court could then be transferred to it, or that removal is needed so a transfer can be considered.
GIVEN `in lower court or tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is pending in a Victorian court other than the Supreme Court, or in a tribunal established by or under an Act
      `related proceeding view` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court finds that your proceeding arises out of or is related to a proceeding pending in the Federal Court, the Family Court or the Supreme Court of another State or Territory, and that, once yours is removed, there would be grounds to transfer that one to the Supreme Court of Victoria
      `consider transfer view` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court finds that yours should be removed so that it can consider transferring it to another court
GIVETH A `Can it be removed into the Supreme Court`
`Can my proceeding be removed into the Supreme Court` MEANS
    `s 8 -- it can be removed into the Supreme Court` `in lower court or tribunal` `related proceeding view` `consider transfer view`

-- s 10 "Where (a) a proceeding is pending in the Supreme Court or the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory;
-- (b) a matter for determination in the proceeding is a matter arising under Part 3-1, Part 3-3 and Part 3-4 of
-- Schedule 2 of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 of the Commonwealth; (c) no matter for determination in the
-- proceeding is a special federal matter; (d) the proceeding is not ... an appeal from a judgment of a court; and (e) a
-- court of the State, other than the Supreme Court, has jurisdiction with respect to all of the matters for
-- determination in the proceeding -- the court referred to in paragraph (a) may, on the application of a party ... or
-- of its own motion, transfer the proceeding to the court referred to in paragraph (e)."
-- (b)'s "and" is read as "or": a matter arising under any one of the three Parts (fork F8; finding 4). "Where": TRUE
-- (it may) when every paragraph holds, FALSE otherwise.
GIVEN `the court it is in` IS A `Court`
      `a matter arises under ACL Part 3-1` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a matter arises under ACL Part 3-3` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a matter arises under ACL Part 3-4` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a matter is a special federal matter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is an appeal from a judgment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a Victorian lower court has jurisdiction over every matter in it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 10 -- it may be sent to a Victorian lower court` `the court it is in` `a matter arises under ACL Part 3-1` `a matter arises under ACL Part 3-3` `a matter arises under ACL Part 3-4` `a matter is a special federal matter` `it is an appeal from a judgment` `a Victorian lower court has jurisdiction over every matter in it` MEANS
    IF      (    `the court it is in` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Victoria`
             OR  `ss 3(2), 5 -- the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory` `the court it is in`)
        AND (`a matter arises under ACL Part 3-1` OR `a matter arises under ACL Part 3-3` OR `a matter arises under ACL Part 3-4`)
        AND NOT (`a matter is a special federal matter`)
        AND NOT (`it is an appeal from a judgment`)
        AND `a Victorian lower court has jurisdiction over every matter in it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

@export My claim in the Supreme Court of Victoria, or of another State or Territory, raises an Australian Consumer Law matter (for example unsafe consumer goods). Can that Supreme Court send it down to a Victorian court below the Supreme Court (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 10)? It may, on a party's application or its own motion, where a matter arises under Part 3-1 (unfair practices), Part 3-3 (safety of consumer goods and product related services) or Part 3-4 (information standards) of the Australian Consumer Law, no matter is a special federal matter, it is not an appeal, and a lower Victorian court has jurisdiction over every matter in it. Part 3-5 (manufacturers' liability for safety defects) is not among the Parts named.
GIVEN `now in` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceeding is in now: the Supreme Court of Victoria; a Victorian court other than the Supreme Court, or a Victorian tribunal; the Supreme Court of another State (including the Northern Territory and the ACT); the Supreme Court of a Territory such as Norfolk Island; the Family Court of Western Australia; the Federal Court of Australia; the Family Court of Australia; or a court outside Australia
      `Part 3-1` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in it arises under Part 3-1 of the Australian Consumer Law (Schedule 2 of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (Cth)): unfair practices
      `Part 3-3` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in it arises under Part 3-3 of the Australian Consumer Law: safety of consumer goods and product related services
      `Part 3-4` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in it arises under Part 3-4 of the Australian Consumer Law: information standards
      `special federal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any matter in it is a special federal matter under the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act
      `appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding is an appeal from a judgment of a court
      `lower court jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Victorian court other than the Supreme Court (for example the County Court) has jurisdiction over every matter in the proceeding
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my consumer law claim be sent to a lower Victorian court` MEANS
    `s 10 -- it may be sent to a Victorian lower court` `now in` `Part 3-1` `Part 3-3` `Part 3-4` `special federal matter` `appeal` `lower court jurisdiction`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 11 -- which law the court applies, and what carries over`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 11(1) "Where it appears to a court that the court will, or will be likely to, in determining a matter ..., be
-- exercising jurisdiction conferred by this Act or by a law of the Commonwealth or a State relating to cross-vesting of
-- jurisdiction -- (a) subject to paragraphs (b) and (c), the court shall ... apply the law in force in the State or
-- Territory in which the court is sitting (including choice of law rules); (b) subject to paragraph (c), if that matter
-- is a right of action arising under a written law of another State or Territory, the court shall ... apply the written
-- and unwritten law of that other State or Territory; and (c) the rules of evidence and procedure ... shall be such as
-- the court considers appropriate in the circumstances, being rules that are applied in a superior court in Australia
-- or in an external Territory."
-- "Where": if the court is not exercising cross-vested jurisdiction, s 11 does not apply (the "no" kind); what law it
-- then applies is outside this Act.
DECLARE `The law the court applies` IS ONE OF
    `s 11 does not apply: the court is not exercising cross-vested jurisdiction in deciding my claim; which law it applies is outside this Act`
    `not covered: s 11 speaks of a court sitting in an Australian State or Territory`
    `the written and unwritten law of the State or Territory under whose written law my right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)), with the rules of evidence and procedure the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
    `the law in force in Victoria, including its choice of law rules, among them the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993 (s 11(1)(a)), with the rules of evidence and procedure the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
    `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)), with the rules of evidence and procedure the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`

GIVEN `the court will exercise cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
      `where the court sits` IS A `Place`
      `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The law the court applies`
`s 11(1) -- the law the court applies` `the court will exercise cross-vested jurisdiction` `where the court sits` `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the court will exercise cross-vested jurisdiction`)
        THEN `s 11 does not apply: the court is not exercising cross-vested jurisdiction in deciding my claim; which law it applies is outside this Act`
        IF NOT (`s 11 -- a State or Territory` `where the court sits`)
        THEN `not covered: s 11 speaks of a court sitting in an Australian State or Territory`
        IF `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory`
        THEN `the written and unwritten law of the State or Territory under whose written law my right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)), with the rules of evidence and procedure the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
        IF `where the court sits` EQUALS `Victoria`
        THEN `the law in force in Victoria, including its choice of law rules, among them the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993 (s 11(1)(a)), with the rules of evidence and procedure the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
        OTHERWISE `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)), with the rules of evidence and procedure the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`

@export Which law will the court apply to my claim once it is hearing it under cross-vesting, for example after a transfer from another State (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 11(1))? Ordinarily the law where the court sits, including its choice of law rules; but if your right of action comes from a statute of another State or Territory (for example a statutory compensation or damages scheme there), that State's or Territory's law. The court picks the rules of evidence and procedure.
GIVEN `cross-vested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds it will (or is likely to) be exercising jurisdiction given by a cross-vesting law in deciding your claim (for example because the claim was transferred to it under s 5, or would be outside its jurisdiction otherwise)
      `court sits in` IS A `Place` @desc Where the court sits: Victoria; another Australian State, the Northern Territory or the ACT; a Territory other than those two (for example Norfolk Island); New Zealand; or a country other than Australia or New Zealand
      `statutory right elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your right of action arises under a statute (written law) of a State or Territory other than the one where the court sits, rather than under the common law
GIVETH A `The law the court applies`
`Which law will the court apply to my claim` MEANS
    `s 11(1) -- the law the court applies` `cross-vested` `court sits in` `statutory right elsewhere`

-- s 11(1) read with the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993 (Victoria), restated narrowly here because a module
-- cannot import another subject: that Act's s 5 "If the substantive law of another place being another State, a
-- Territory or New Zealand, is to govern a claim before a court of this State, a limitation law of that place is to be
-- regarded as part of that substantive law and applied accordingly by the court." (Its s 4 excludes only proceedings
-- instituted before 23 November 1993, not restated.) Which place's substantive law governs is the court's choice of law
-- decision (asked). A limitation law of the place in s 11(1)(b) is part of its "written and unwritten law" (fork F9).
DECLARE `Whose limitation law` IS ONE OF
    `s 11 does not apply: the court is not exercising cross-vested jurisdiction; for a claim heard in Victoria in the court's own jurisdiction, the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993 applies directly`
    `not covered: s 11 speaks of a court sitting in an Australian State or Territory, so it says nothing of the limitation law`
    `the limitation law of the State or Territory whose statute gives my right of action, as part of its written and unwritten law (s 11(1)(b))`
    `the limitation law of the place whose law governs my claim: s 11(1)(a) applies Victorian law with its choice of law rules, and the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993 s 5 makes that place's limitation law part of its substantive law`
    `Victoria's own limitation law: s 11(1)(a) applies Victorian law, and Victorian law governs my claim`
    `not covered: s 11(1)(a) applies Victorian law with its choice of law rules, but the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993 reaches only another State, a Territory or New Zealand; for another country's law the answer is outside both Acts`
    `the law where the court sits decides it, with its own choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); that law is outside this Act`

GIVEN `the court will exercise cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
      `where the court sits` IS A `Place`
      `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the place whose law governs my claim` IS A `Place`
GIVETH A `Whose limitation law`
`s 11(1) with CLLPA s 5 -- whose limitation law` `the court will exercise cross-vested jurisdiction` `where the court sits` `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory` `the place whose law governs my claim` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the court will exercise cross-vested jurisdiction`)
        THEN `s 11 does not apply: the court is not exercising cross-vested jurisdiction; for a claim heard in Victoria in the court's own jurisdiction, the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993 applies directly`
        IF NOT (`s 11 -- a State or Territory` `where the court sits`)
        THEN `not covered: s 11 speaks of a court sitting in an Australian State or Territory, so it says nothing of the limitation law`
        IF `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory`
        THEN `the limitation law of the State or Territory whose statute gives my right of action, as part of its written and unwritten law (s 11(1)(b))`
        IF NOT (`where the court sits` EQUALS `Victoria`)
        THEN `the law where the court sits decides it, with its own choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); that law is outside this Act`
        IF `the place whose law governs my claim` EQUALS `Victoria`
        THEN `Victoria's own limitation law: s 11(1)(a) applies Victorian law, and Victorian law governs my claim`
        IF `the place whose law governs my claim` EQUALS `a country other than Australia or New Zealand`
        THEN `not covered: s 11(1)(a) applies Victorian law with its choice of law rules, but the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993 reaches only another State, a Territory or New Zealand; for another country's law the answer is outside both Acts`
        OTHERWISE `the limitation law of the place whose law governs my claim: s 11(1)(a) applies Victorian law with its choice of law rules, and the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993 s 5 makes that place's limitation law part of its substantive law`

@export Whose time limit (limitation period) will apply to my claim when a court is hearing it under cross-vesting (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 11(1), read with the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993 (Victoria) s 5)? If your right of action comes from another State's or Territory's statute, that place's law, limitation law included. Otherwise a court in Victoria applies Victorian law with its choice of law rules, under which the limitation law of the place whose law governs the claim (another State, a Territory or New Zealand) applies. Which place's law governs is for the court: give it as an input.
GIVEN `cross-vested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds it will (or is likely to) be exercising jurisdiction given by a cross-vesting law in deciding your claim
      `court sits in` IS A `Place` @desc Where the court sits: Victoria; another Australian State, the Northern Territory or the ACT; a Territory other than those two (for example Norfolk Island); New Zealand; or a country other than Australia or New Zealand
      `statutory right elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your right of action arises under a statute (written law) of a State or Territory other than the one where the court sits
      `governing place` IS A `Place` @desc The place whose substantive law governs your claim under the choice of law rules (for an injury, usually the place where it happened), from the same list
GIVETH A `Whose limitation law`
`Whose limitation law applies after cross-vesting` MEANS
    `s 11(1) with CLLPA s 5 -- whose limitation law` `cross-vested` `court sits in` `statutory right elsewhere` `governing place`

-- s 11(3) "Where a proceeding is transferred or removed to a court ... the transferee court shall deal with the
-- proceeding as if, subject to any order of the transferee court, the steps that had been taken for the purposes of the
-- proceeding in the transferor court (including the making of an order), or similar steps, had been taken in the
-- transferee court." TRUE where transferred or removed and the new court has not ordered otherwise; FALSE otherwise
-- (fork F10 for "subject to any order").
GIVEN `it was transferred or removed to the new court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the new court has ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 11(3) -- earlier steps count in the new court` `it was transferred or removed to the new court` `the new court has ordered otherwise` MEANS
    IF `it was transferred or removed to the new court` AND NOT (`the new court has ordered otherwise`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

@export My proceeding has been transferred or removed to another court. Do the steps already taken in the old court (pleadings, orders, discovery and the like) count in the new court (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 11(3))? Yes: the new court deals with the proceeding as if those steps, or similar steps, had been taken before it, unless it orders otherwise.
GIVEN `transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding has been transferred or removed to another court under a cross-vesting law
      `ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the new court has made an order that the earlier steps (or a step you ask about) are not to count
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do the steps already taken count in the new court` MEANS
    `s 11(3) -- earlier steps count in the new court` `transferred` `ordered otherwise`

-- s 12 "Where a proceeding is transferred or removed to a court, that court may make an order as to costs that relate to
-- the conduct of the proceeding before the transfer or removal if those costs have not already been dealt with by
-- another court." TRUE (it may) where transferred and the costs not dealt with; FALSE otherwise ("where", "if").
GIVEN `it was transferred or removed to the new court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `another court has already dealt with those costs` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12 -- the new court may order the earlier costs` `it was transferred or removed to the new court` `another court has already dealt with those costs` MEANS
    IF `it was transferred or removed to the new court` AND NOT (`another court has already dealt with those costs`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

@export My proceeding has been transferred or removed to another court. Can the new court make orders about the costs of the proceeding before the transfer (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 12)? Yes, if another court has not already dealt with those costs. What it orders is for the court.
GIVEN `transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding has been transferred or removed to another court
      `costs dealt with` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another court (for example the court it left) has already dealt with the costs of the proceeding before the transfer
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the new court deal with the costs from before the transfer` MEANS
    `s 12 -- the new court may order the earlier costs` `transferred` `costs dealt with`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 13, 14 -- challenging a transfer, and enforcing the judgment`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 13 "An appeal does not lie from a decision of a court (a) in relation to the transfer or removal of a proceeding
-- under this Act; or (b) as to which rules of evidence and procedure are to be applied pursuant to section 11(1)."
-- A flat negative: FALSE for (a) and (b). "In relation to" covers a refusal to transfer as much as a transfer (fork
-- F11). A transfer under the Commonwealth's or another State's Act, and every other decision: NOTHING (this Act is
-- silent; s 7 says where an appeal on the merits goes).
DECLARE `The decision I want to appeal` IS ONE OF
    `a decision to transfer or remove my proceeding under this Act, or not to`
    `a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)`
    `a decision on transferring my proceeding under the Commonwealth's or another State's cross-vesting Act`
    `another decision, such as the judgment on my claim`

GIVEN `the decision` IS A `The decision I want to appeal`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 13 -- an appeal lies` `the decision` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the decision`
    WHEN `a decision to transfer or remove my proceeding under this Act, or not to` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

@export Can I appeal against the court's decision (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 13)? No appeal lies from a decision about transferring or removing a proceeding under this Act, or about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1). For other decisions this section says nothing: see s 7 for where an appeal on the merits goes.
GIVEN `decision` IS A `The decision I want to appeal` @desc The decision you want to appeal: a decision to transfer or remove your proceeding under this Act, or not to; a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1); a decision on transferring it under the Commonwealth's or another State's cross-vesting Act; or another decision, such as the judgment on your claim
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I appeal the decision` MEANS
    `s 13 -- an appeal lies` `decision`

-- s 14(2) "A judgment of the Supreme Court that is given, in whole or in part, in the exercise of jurisdiction conferred
-- by a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction is enforceable in the State as if the judgment had been
-- given entirely in the exercise of the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court apart from any such law." TRUE for such a
-- judgment; NOTHING for any other (the section's subject; its ordinary enforcement is outside this Act).
GIVEN `the Supreme Court's judgment was given at least partly in cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 14(2) -- enforceable as an ordinary Supreme Court judgment` `the Supreme Court's judgment was given at least partly in cross-vested jurisdiction` MEANS
    IF `the Supreme Court's judgment was given at least partly in cross-vested jurisdiction`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

@export I have a judgment of the Supreme Court of Victoria given wholly or partly under cross-vested jurisdiction. Can it be enforced in Victoria like any other Supreme Court judgment (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Victoria) s 14(2))? Yes: it is enforceable in Victoria as if given entirely in the Supreme Court's own jurisdiction. No answer for other judgments, which this section does not deal with.
GIVEN `cross-vested judgment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court of Victoria's judgment was given, wholly or partly, in jurisdiction conferred by a cross-vesting law
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can a cross-vesting judgment be enforced in Victoria` MEANS
    `s 14(2) -- enforceable as an ordinary Supreme Court judgment` `cross-vested judgment`
